1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,639
<font color="#FFFFFF">Reinforcement learning promises optimal decisions,</font>

2
00:00:03,719 --> 00:00:07,951
<font color="#FFFFFF">but almost every algorithm quietly assumes the
world never changes.</font>

3
00:00:08,031 --> 00:00:12,264
<font color="#FFFFFF">In real settings like mobile health, traffic
control, and robotics,</font>

4
00:00:12,344 --> 00:00:18,058
<font color="#FFFFFF">that stationarity assumption breaks down over time
and quietly poisons the learned policy.</font>

5
00:00:18,138 --> 00:00:23,529
<font color="#FFFFFF">This paper introduces a doubly robust statistical
test that checks whether an offline</font>

6
00:00:23,609 --> 00:00:26,555
<font color="#FFFFFF">Markov decision process is actually stationary,</font>

7
00:00:26,635 --> 00:00:28,550
<font color="#FFFFFF">and pinpoints where it changes.</font>

8
00:00:28,630 --> 00:00:33,764
<font color="#FFFFFF">The test pairs modern machine learning estimators
with semiparametric statistics,</font>

9
00:00:33,844 --> 00:00:40,008
<font color="#FFFFFF">so it controls false alarms while staying powerful
even when the state space is high dimensional.</font>

10
00:00:40,088 --> 00:00:43,549
<font color="#FFFFFF">Across four simulations and a real intern health study,</font>

11
00:00:43,629 --> 00:00:51,080
<font color="#FFFFFF">it detects change points others miss and lets
policies recover the reward that non stationarity would otherwise cost.</font>

12
00:00:51,460 --> 00:00:55,862
<font color="#FFFFFF">Reinforcement learning agents are trained to find
the optimal policy,</font>

13
00:00:55,942 --> 00:01:02,032
<font color="#FFFFFF">but nearly every algorithm leans on one fragile
assumption: that the environment never changes.</font>

14
00:01:02,112 --> 00:01:04,371
<font color="#FFFFFF">This is the stationarity assumption,</font>

15
00:01:04,451 --> 00:01:09,307
<font color="#FFFFFF">requiring the state transition and reward functions
to stay fixed over time.</font>

16
00:01:09,387 --> 00:01:11,710
<font color="#FFFFFF">In the real world, that rarely holds.</font>

17
00:01:11,790 --> 00:01:16,452
<font color="#FFFFFF">Robotics, healthcare, and digital marketing all
drift over long horizons,</font>

18
00:01:16,532 --> 00:01:21,388
<font color="#FFFFFF">and a policy learned as if the world were frozen
quietly becomes suboptimal,</font>

19
00:01:21,468 --> 00:01:22,882
<font color="#FFFFFF">sometimes even harmful.</font>

20
00:01:22,962 --> 00:01:28,663
<font color="#FFFFFF">The problem this paper tackles is how to reliably
tell whether an offline decision-making</font>

21
00:01:28,743 --> 00:01:33,210
<font color="#FFFFFF">system is actually stationary before you trust the
policy it produced.</font>

22
00:01:33,590 --> 00:01:35,117
<font color="#FFFFFF">Why does this matter now?</font>

23
00:01:35,197 --> 00:01:37,238
<font color="#FFFFFF">Consider the Intern Health Study,</font>

24
00:01:37,318 --> 00:01:44,757
<font color="#FFFFFF">a year-long mobile-health trial that nudges
first-year physicians toward healthier habits through push notifications.</font>

25
00:01:44,837 --> 00:01:49,898
<font color="#FFFFFF">The effect of those nudges wanes over time, a
textbook case of non-stationarity.</font>

26
00:01:49,978 --> 00:01:53,048
<font color="#FFFFFF">Similar drift shows up in traffic signal control,</font>

27
00:01:53,128 --> 00:01:56,775
<font color="#FFFFFF">where flow patterns swing between peak and off-peak hours.</font>

28
00:01:56,855 --> 00:02:03,587
<font color="#FFFFFF">Ignoring these shifts leads to policies that send
prompts at the wrong moments and erode long-term reward.</font>

29
00:02:03,667 --> 00:02:07,893
<font color="#FFFFFF">Prior stationarity tests either demand knowledge of
the true model,</font>

30
00:02:07,973 --> 00:02:12,456
<font color="#FFFFFF">or fall back on linear approximations that collapse
in high dimensions,</font>

31
00:02:12,536 --> 00:02:15,220
<font color="#FFFFFF">leaving a real gap for modern applications.</font>

32
00:02:15,600 --> 00:02:18,352
<font color="#FFFFFF">The paper's core contribution is a model-based,</font>

33
00:02:18,432 --> 00:02:23,656
<font color="#FFFFFF">doubly robust procedure that tests the stationarity
assumption and locates change points</font>

34
00:02:23,736 --> 00:02:25,705
<font color="#FFFFFF">in offline reinforcement learning.</font>

35
00:02:25,785 --> 00:02:31,068
<font color="#FFFFFF">Doubly robust means the test controls the type-one
error as long as either the transition</font>

36
00:02:31,148 --> 00:02:35,528
<font color="#FFFFFF">function or the marginal state-action distribution
is correctly specified,</font>

37
00:02:35,608 --> 00:02:39,445
<font color="#FFFFFF">so you get valid inference even when one nuisance
model is wrong.</font>

38
00:02:39,525 --> 00:02:40,711
<font color="#FFFFFF">On top of the method,</font>

39
00:02:40,791 --> 00:02:46,496
<font color="#FFFFFF">the authors prove size control and double
robustness under a bidirectional asymptotic framework,</font>

40
00:02:46,576 --> 00:02:51,920
<font color="#FFFFFF">where either the number of trajectories or the
length of the horizon may grow to infinity,</font>

41
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:56,440
<font color="#FFFFFF">and they supply a Gaussian multiplier bootstrap to
compute honest p-values.</font>

42
00:02:56,820 --> 00:02:58,465
<font color="#FFFFFF">Here is how the test works.</font>

43
00:02:58,545 --> 00:03:00,445
<font color="#FFFFFF">At each candidate change point,</font>

44
00:03:00,525 --> 00:03:06,832
<font color="#FFFFFF">it compares the pooled transition dynamics before
and after that time using a CUSUM-style statistic.</font>

45
00:03:06,912 --> 00:03:12,900
<font color="#FFFFFF">Naively plugging modern machine-learning estimators
into this comparison introduces heavy bias,</font>

46
00:03:12,980 --> 00:03:16,030
<font color="#FFFFFF">so the authors add a mean-zero augmentation term,</font>

47
00:03:16,110 --> 00:03:18,969
<font color="#FFFFFF">producing a doubly robust estimating function.</font>

48
00:03:19,049 --> 00:03:24,654
<font color="#FFFFFF">That lets flexible models like neural networks and
random forests estimate the transition</font>

49
00:03:24,734 --> 00:03:28,614
<font color="#FFFFFF">and state-action distributions without spoiling the
inference.</font>

50
00:03:28,694 --> 00:03:35,129
<font color="#FFFFFF">Sample splitting and cross-fitting normalize the
statistic across candidate points and test functions,</font>

51
00:03:35,209 --> 00:03:41,389
<font color="#FFFFFF">and a Gaussian multiplier bootstrap approximates
its null distribution to yield the final p-value.</font>

52
00:03:41,469 --> 00:03:45,157
<font color="#FFFFFF">The maximum over time and test functions of the
normalized,</font>

53
00:03:45,237 --> 00:03:48,990
<font color="#FFFFFF">CUSUM-weighted statistic gives the test statistic
gamma-hat.</font>

54
00:03:49,370 --> 00:03:53,852
<font color="#FFFFFF">The method is stress-tested across four numerical
studies and one real dataset.</font>

55
00:03:53,932 --> 00:03:58,414
<font color="#FFFFFF">A discrete-state toy example illustrates the double
robustness property directly.</font>

56
00:03:58,494 --> 00:04:02,977
<font color="#FFFFFF">High-dimensional synthetic data pushes the state
dimension from one up to thirty and pits</font>

57
00:04:03,057 --> 00:04:07,539
<font color="#FFFFFF">the test against two existing baselines,</font>

58
00:04:07,619 --> 00:04:12,101
<font color="#FFFFFF">ODCP and CUSUM-RL.</font>

59
00:04:12,181 --> 00:04:16,663
<font color="#FFFFFF">A four-by-four grid world shows how detecting the
change point improves policy learning,</font>

60
00:04:16,743 --> 00:04:21,226
<font color="#FFFFFF">and a batch-online semi-synthetic study mimics the
structure of the real trial.</font>

61
00:04:21,306 --> 00:04:25,788
<font color="#FFFFFF">Finally, the authors apply the test to the Intern
Health Study,</font>

62
00:04:25,868 --> 00:04:30,350
<font color="#FFFFFF">a twenty-one-week mobile-health micro-randomized
trial of medical interns in the United States.</font>

63
00:04:30,730 --> 00:04:34,533
<font color="#FFFFFF">The headline finding is about robustness to
dimensionality.</font>

64
00:04:34,613 --> 00:04:38,416
<font color="#FFFFFF">In every setting, the proposed test keeps the
type-one error at the nominal level,</font>

65
00:04:38,496 --> 00:04:42,299
<font color="#FFFFFF">and crucially it still pinpoints the true change
point even when the state has ten,</font>

66
00:04:42,379 --> 00:04:46,182
<font color="#FFFFFF">twenty, or thirty dimensions.</font>

67
00:04:46,262 --> 00:04:50,065
<font color="#FFFFFF">By contrast,</font>

68
00:04:50,145 --> 00:04:53,948
<font color="#FFFFFF">the CUSUM-RL baseline only recovers the change
point when the state is one-dimensional,</font>

69
00:04:54,028 --> 00:04:57,831
<font color="#FFFFFF">and the ODCP method fails to control the type-one
error at all in high dimensions.</font>

70
00:04:57,911 --> 00:05:01,714
<font color="#FFFFFF">The toy example confirms the double robustness in
action: size and power hold as long</font>

71
00:05:01,794 --> 00:05:05,597
<font color="#FFFFFF">as at least one of the two nuisance models is right,</font>

72
00:05:05,677 --> 00:05:09,480
<font color="#FFFFFF">with the strongest power when both are correct.</font>

73
00:05:09,860 --> 00:05:15,240
<font color="#FFFFFF">The most informative ablation varies the model
misspecification level from mild to severe,</font>

74
00:05:15,320 --> 00:05:17,243
<font color="#FFFFFF">across five hundred replications.</font>

75
00:05:17,323 --> 00:05:22,400
<font color="#FFFFFF">It shows the empirical size staying pinned at the
nominal level whenever at least one</font>

76
00:05:22,480 --> 00:05:25,737
<font color="#FFFFFF">of the two nuisance models remains correctly specified,</font>

77
00:05:25,817 --> 00:05:29,134
<font color="#FFFFFF">a clean demonstration of the double robustness property.</font>

78
00:05:29,214 --> 00:05:32,956
<font color="#FFFFFF">A second sweep varies kappa, the length of the
tested interval,</font>

79
00:05:33,036 --> 00:05:38,235
<font color="#FFFFFF">to actually locate the single change point: the
test correctly holds its size while the</font>

80
00:05:38,315 --> 00:05:44,120
<font color="#FFFFFF">null is true and its power climbs once the interval
crosses the true change point at twenty-five.</font>

81
00:05:44,500 --> 00:05:46,442
<font color="#FFFFFF">A few numbers anchor the impact.</font>

82
00:05:46,522 --> 00:05:52,447
<font color="#FFFFFF">The test sustains correct change-point detection
all the way up to a state dimension of thirty,</font>

83
00:05:52,527 --> 00:05:56,175
<font color="#FFFFFF">whereas the closest competitor works only at
dimension one.</font>

84
00:05:56,255 --> 00:06:03,507
<font color="#FFFFFF">It maintains the nominal five-percent significance
level across all four simulations and both real-data specialties.</font>

85
00:06:03,587 --> 00:06:06,098
<font color="#FFFFFF">And on the real Intern Health Study data,</font>

86
00:06:06,178 --> 00:06:11,028
<font color="#FFFFFF">it flags a genuine change point for Internal
Medicine interns at week sixteen,</font>

87
00:06:11,108 --> 00:06:15,073
<font color="#FFFFFF">while correctly finding no change for the Family
Practice group.</font>

88
00:06:15,153 --> 00:06:21,140
<font color="#FFFFFF">These results come from thousands of bootstrap
samples and hundreds of replications per setting.</font>

89
00:06:21,520 --> 00:06:22,892
<font color="#FFFFFF">The takeaway is simple.</font>

90
00:06:22,972 --> 00:06:29,144
<font color="#FFFFFF">By marrying the flexibility of modern machine
learning with the rigor of semiparametric statistics,</font>

91
00:06:29,224 --> 00:06:35,142
<font color="#FFFFFF">this doubly robust CUSUM test reliably flags when
an offline reinforcement-learning environment</font>

92
00:06:35,222 --> 00:06:36,595
<font color="#FFFFFF">stops being stationary,</font>

93
00:06:36,675 --> 00:06:41,267
<font color="#FFFFFF">even in high dimensions, so that policies can be
relearned on the correct,</font>

94
00:06:41,347 --> 00:06:42,972
<font color="#FFFFFF">stationary segment of data.</font>

95
00:06:43,052 --> 00:06:45,056
<font color="#FFFFFF">When some homogeneity is present,</font>

96
00:06:45,136 --> 00:06:50,486
<font color="#FFFFFF">detecting and adapting to change points recovers
near-oracle reward that stationary or</font>

97
00:06:50,566 --> 00:06:53,770
<font color="#FFFFFF">sliding-window strategies simply leave on the table.</font>
